# Patient Treatment and Transport (NREMT EMR test plan, 2025: 20% to 24%). Job tasks: manage
# airway, ventilation and oxygenation; manage the cardiovascular and circulatory system; manage
# motion restriction; administer medication; manage special populations; manage transport;
# communicate pertinent information.
# EMR scope (National EMS Scope of Practice Model 2019 with change notices 1.0 and 2.0): CPR and
# AED, oxygen by nasal cannula and nonrebreather, BVM, cervical collar, manual cervical
# stabilization, manual extremity stabilization and splinting, emergency moves, eye irrigation,
# assisted uncomplicated childbirth, intranasal unit-dose and auto-injector naloxone, antidote
# auto-injectors. NOT EMR in the national model: epinephrine auto-injector, aspirin, oral glucose,
# inhaled medications, traction splint, long spine board, seated SMR device, patient restraint,
# complicated childbirth, being the attendant during ambulance transport.
# Clinical figures: NASEMSO National Model EMS Clinical Guidelines v3.0 (cardiac arrest,
# opioid poisoning, spinal care, burns, hyperthermia, hypothermia, childbirth, neonatal care) and
# the 2025 AHA guideline releases.
ITEMS = [
    # ---------- Practice Test 1 ----------
    S(1, 2, "CPR quality", "Compressions on an adult should be at least 2 inches deep. What is the upper limit?",
      ["About 2.4 inches", "About 1.5 inches", "About 3.5 inches", "About 4 inches"],
      "Compress an adult at least 2 inches (5 cm) but not more than about 2.4 inches (6 cm), at 100 to 120 a minute. Deeper compressions add injury without more blood flow."),
    S(1, 2, "AED", "The AED says shock advised. What do you do?",
      ["Clear everyone, then shock", "Check a pulse before shocking", "Give two breaths, then shock", "Remove the pads and reapply them"],
      "Clear everyone from the patient, press the shock button, then start compressions right away for 2 minutes before the AED analyzes again. A pulse check before the shock only delays it."),
    S(1, 3, "CPR scope", "Under the national model, which resuscitation tool is listed at the EMT level and above, not for EMRs?",
      ["Mechanical CPR device", "Automated external defibrillator", "Pocket mask with a one-way valve", "Bag-valve mask"],
      "The 2019 national model lists CPR, the AED, mouth-to-mask breathing and the BVM at every level from EMR up. Mechanical CPR devices start at the EMT level."),
    S(1, 2, "Oxygen delivery", "A short-of-breath adult has clearly low oxygen signs and is breathing adequately. Which device gives the most oxygen within EMR scope?",
      ["Nonrebreather mask at 15 L/min", "Nasal cannula at 2 L/min for comfort", "Simple face mask at 6 L/min", "CPAP mask set by an EMT partner"],
      "A nonrebreather at 10 to 15 L/min delivers a high concentration of oxygen and is within EMR scope. Simple face masks and CPAP are listed at the EMT level in the national model."),
    S(1, 2, "Naloxone", "Which naloxone routes are within EMR scope under the national model?",
      ["Intranasal unit dose and auto-injector", "Intravenous push through a new line", "Intraosseous", "Nebulized"],
      "The national scope model lets EMRs give naloxone for suspected opioid overdose by unit-dosed intranasal device or auto-injector. IV and IO routes are AEMT and above."),
    S(1, 3, "Epinephrine scope", "A woman with known bee allergy has hives and trouble breathing. Under the national model, what is the EMR's role with an epinephrine auto-injector?",
      ["EMT and above; states may add EMRs", "Any EMR may always inject it", "EMRs may give it only IV", "No EMS level may give epinephrine"],
      "The 2019 national model lists the epinephrine auto-injector for anaphylaxis at the EMT level and above, though some states add it for EMRs. Support the airway, call ALS early and follow your state and medical director."),
    S(1, 2, "Spinal motion restriction", "A conscious driver complains of neck pain after a rollover. What is within EMR scope?",
      ["Manual stabilization and a cervical collar", "Strapping him to a long spine board on scene", "A seated extrication device such as a KED", "Leaving him unsupported"],
      "The national model adds the cervical collar and manual cervical stabilization at the EMR level. Long boards and seated devices such as the KED are EMT-level tools."),
    S(1, 2, "Splinting", "Before you splint an injured wrist, why take off the patient's rings and watch?",
      ["Swelling can trap them and cut off blood flow", "They interfere with the AED", "They must be logged for the hospital", "They make the splint too heavy"],
      "Injured limbs swell, and a ring or watch can become a tourniquet that cuts off circulation to the hand. Remove them early and give them to the patient or family."),
    S(1, 2, "Burns", "A cook has a fresh burn on his forearm from hot oil. What should you do first?",
      ["Cool it with clean running water", "Apply butter or oil to soothe it", "Pop the blisters", "Pack it in ice"],
      "Cool water stops the burning and eases pain. Ice can deepen the injury, and blisters should be left intact. Then cover the burn with a dry, clean dressing."),
    M(1, 2, "Childbirth", "During an uncomplicated delivery, which actions are right? Select two.",
      ["Support the baby's head as it emerges", "Dry and warm the newborn", "Pull the baby out by the head", "Push the head back in to wait", "Cut the cord before the baby is out"],
      "Guide and support the head gently, then dry, warm and stimulate the newborn. Never pull on the baby or push the head back unless a cord problem needs it under higher-level care."),
    O(1, 2, "AED use", "Put the steps of using an AED on an adult in cardiac arrest in order.",
      ["Turn on the AED", "Attach the pads to the bare chest", "Stand clear while it analyzes", "Deliver the shock if advised", "Resume compressions at once"],
      "Power the AED, place the pads, keep everyone clear while it checks the rhythm, shock when told to and go straight back to compressions."),
    X(1, 2, "EMR medications", "Under the national model, is each medication within EMR scope?",
      ["Intranasal naloxone", "Oral aspirin for chest pain", "Oral glucose", "Agency antidote auto-injector", "Inhaled albuterol"],
      ["Within EMR scope", "EMT and above"], [0, 1, 1, 0, 1],
      "EMRs may give naloxone by intranasal unit dose or auto-injector and antidote auto-injectors for chemical exposures. Aspirin, oral glucose and inhaled bronchodilators are listed at the EMT level and above."),
    S(1, 2, "Handoff", "Which handoff to the arriving EMT is most useful?",
      ["Complaint, findings, care and changes", "A guess at the diagnosis only", "The patient's address and phone number", "How long you waited for them to arrive on scene"],
      "A short, organized handoff gives the next crew what they need: who the patient is, the complaint, what you found, what you did and how the patient has responded."),
    # ---------- Practice Test 2 ----------
    S(2, 2, "Child CPR", "For a small child in cardiac arrest, which hands may you use for compressions?",
      ["One or two hands, as needed", "Only two fingers", "Only both thumbs", "Only one finger"],
      "Use one or two hands, whatever it takes to press about one third of the depth of the chest. Finger and thumb techniques are for infants."),
    S(2, 2, "Infant CPR", "Which method of infant chest compression does the 2025 AHA guidance drop?",
      ["Two fingers on the breastbone", "Two thumbs with hands encircling the chest", "The heel of one hand", "Compressions with a full ratio and breaths"],
      "The 2025 guidance dropped the two-finger technique because it often fails to reach the right depth. Rescuers use two thumbs with the hands around the chest, or the heel of one hand."),
    S(2, 2, "CPR ratio", "You and a second rescuer give CPR to a 4-year-old. What ratio do you use?",
      ["15 compressions to 2 breaths", "30 compressions to 2 breaths", "5 compressions to 1 breath at any age", "Compressions only, with no breaths at all"],
      "With two rescuers on a child or infant, use 15:2. A single rescuer uses 30:2 for every age."),
    S(2, 2, "Oxygen delivery", "A patient with mild shortness of breath will not tolerate a mask. What can you use?",
      ["Nasal cannula at 1 to 6 L/min", "Nonrebreather at 2 L/min", "A BVM held loosely over the face", "Oxygen blown at the face from a tube"],
      "A nasal cannula gives low to moderate oxygen at 1 to 6 L/min and is easier to tolerate. A nonrebreather needs a high flow to keep the bag full."),
    S(2, 3, "Naloxone", "After intranasal naloxone, an overdose patient wakes, breathes well and wants to leave. What should you know?",
      ["Naloxone can wear off before the opioid does", "He can no longer stop breathing", "A second dose is never needed after waking", "He is safe to drive himself home after an hour"],
      "Many opioids last longer than naloxone, so breathing can slow again. Keep monitoring, encourage EMS transport and be ready to support breathing."),
    S(2, 2, "Heat stroke", "Which field method cools a patient with heat stroke the fastest?",
      ["Cold-water immersion of the body", "A cool drink sipped slowly", "A fan with dry air only", "Ice packs on the wrists alone"],
      "Immersing the body in cold water removes heat fastest and is the preferred field method where available. Ice packs and fanning help, but more slowly."),
    S(2, 2, "Hypothermia", "A man pulled from snow is cold and drowsy but breathing. What should you do?",
      ["Handle him gently and warm him", "Rub his arms and legs hard to warm them", "Have him walk around to warm up his legs", "Give him an alcoholic drink to warm his core"],
      "A very cold heart is irritable, so rough handling can trigger a dangerous rhythm. Move him gently, remove wet clothing, insulate him and warm the trunk."),
    M(2, 2, "Transport", "Under the national model, which statements about an EMR and ambulance transport are true? Select two.",
      ["An EMR may assist with care in the ambulance", "An EMT or higher must be in the patient compartment", "An EMR may be the only attendant", "An EMR must drive with lights on", "An EMR may not touch the patient en route"],
      "The national model says an EMR may help with care during transport, but an EMT or higher must be in the patient compartment and responsible for care."),
    O(2, 2, "Cervical collar", "Put the steps of placing a cervical collar on a seated patient in order.",
      ["Hold the head still in line", "Check movement and feeling in the hands", "Size the collar", "Slide the back of the collar behind the neck", "Fasten the front and recheck"],
      "Manual stabilization comes first and continues throughout. Check function, measure and size the collar, place it and fasten it, then recheck movement and feeling."),
    X(2, 2, "Splinting scope", "Under the national model, is each device or skill within EMR scope?",
      ["Rigid splint for the forearm", "Traction splint for the thigh", "Manual extremity stabilization", "Long spine board", "Cervical collar"],
      ["Within EMR scope", "EMT and above"], [0, 1, 0, 1, 0],
      "EMRs may stabilize an injured limb by hand, splint it and place a cervical collar. Traction splints and long spine boards are listed at the EMT level and above."),
    S(2, 2, "Eye injury", "You flush a chemical from one eye. Which way should the water run?",
      ["Away from the other eye", "Across the nose into the other eye", "Straight down onto the cornea", "Into the ear on that side"],
      "Tilt the head so water runs from the inner corner outward and away from the unaffected eye, so the chemical is not washed into it. Eye irrigation is within EMR scope."),
    S(2, 2, "Older patient", "An 82-year-old fell at home and seems slightly confused. Her family says this is new. What should you think?",
      ["New confusion may signal a serious problem", "Confusion is normal at her age", "She is likely only tired", "No care is needed if she can stand"],
      "A sudden change in mental status in an older adult is never just age. It can mean a head injury, low oxygen, infection or stroke. Report the change and its timing to EMS."),
    S(2, 3, "Opioid overdose", "Under the national model, after giving intranasal naloxone to a patient who is still not breathing, what must you keep doing?",
      ["Ventilate with a BVM and oxygen", "Wait quietly for the drug to work", "Give him a cold shower to wake him up", "Sit him up and slap his face"],
      "Naloxone takes minutes to work and may not be enough. Keep ventilating until breathing returns and start CPR if he has no pulse. A shower or slaps waste time."),
    # ---------- Drill only ----------
    S(0, 2, "CPR quality", "Which error keeps the chest from recoiling fully between compressions?",
      ["Leaning on the chest", "Locking your elbows", "Counting out loud", "Kneeling close to the patient"],
      "Resting your weight on the chest between compressions stops it from coming all the way back up, so the heart cannot refill well. Locked elbows and counting aloud are good technique."),
    S(0, 2, "Interruptions", "Your partner wants to stop compressions every 30 seconds to feel for a pulse. What should you say?",
      ["Keep going until the 2-minute check", "Good idea, check every 30 seconds", "Check after every 5 compressions", "Stop compressions until EMS arrives"],
      "Every pause stops blood flow. Keep pauses under 10 seconds and check only at the planned rhythm and pulse check about every 2 minutes."),
    S(0, 2, "Rescuer swap", "Why should rescuers trade the compression role regularly?",
      ["Tired rescuers push too shallow", "It lets the patient rest", "It keeps the AED charged", "It is required to count breaths"],
      "Compression depth and rate drop as rescuers tire, often before they notice. Switching about every 2 minutes, at the AED analysis, keeps compressions strong."),
    S(0, 2, "AED pads", "The AED keeps saying check pads because a man's thick chest hair stops them sticking. What should you do?",
      ["Remove the hair, then use new pads", "Press the pads harder and keep going", "Move the pads onto his belly", "Stop and continue CPR without the AED"],
      "Pads need skin contact. Shave quickly where the pads go, or pull the first set off to strip the hair and place a spare set. Keep pauses in compressions short."),
    S(0, 2, "Implanted device", "You see a bulge under the skin below an arrest patient's collarbone. How should you place the AED pad?",
      ["Away from the bulge", "Directly over the bulge", "On the arm instead", "Skip the AED entirely"],
      "The bulge is likely a pacemaker or implanted defibrillator. Place the pad so it is not directly over the device and use the AED as usual."),
    S(0, 2, "Oxygen safety", "Which practice is safe around oxygen?",
      ["Keep it away from open flames", "Store cylinders lying loose in a vehicle", "Grease the regulator threads", "Let a patient smoke with a cannula on"],
      "Oxygen feeds fire. Keep flames and sparks away, secure cylinders and never put oil or grease on the fittings."),
    S(0, 2, "Pregnant patient", "Why does a woman late in pregnancy often feel faint lying flat on her back?",
      ["The uterus presses on a large vein", "The baby uses all her oxygen", "Her blood sugar falls at once", "Her lungs fill with fluid"],
      "The heavy uterus presses on the large vein that returns blood to the heart, so less blood is pumped out. Turning her onto her left side relieves it."),
    S(0, 2, "Newborn care", "A newborn is still not breathing well after drying and rubbing. What should you do next?",
      ["Ventilate with a newborn-sized BVM", "Hold the baby upside down by the feet", "Slap the baby's feet hard", "Wait 5 minutes for crying to start"],
      "If drying and stimulation do not start good breathing, ventilations with a BVM sized for a newborn are the next step. Most newborns who need help need breathing support."),
    S(0, 3, "Complicated delivery", "During a delivery, a loop of umbilical cord comes out before the baby. Under the national model, what is your role?",
      ["Call for higher-level help", "Push the cord back in yourself", "Pull the baby out quickly by the shoulders", "Cut the cord at once"],
      "Assisted complicated delivery is listed at the EMT level and above in the national model. Get the highest level of care coming now and help them; a prolapsed cord needs urgent hospital care."),
    S(0, 2, "Splinting", "A bone end is poking through the skin of the shin. How should you handle the wound?",
      ["Cover it with a sterile dressing and splint", "Push the bone back under the skin, then bandage", "Rinse the bone with soap", "Leave it open to the air"],
      "Cover an open fracture with a sterile dressing to limit contamination, control bleeding without pressing on the bone, and splint it as it lies."),
    S(0, 2, "Dislocation", "A man's shoulder looks out of place after a fall. What should you do?",
      ["Splint it in the position found", "Pull the arm to put it back", "Have him move it in circles", "Wrap the hand to the chest very tightly"],
      "Splint a suspected dislocation as found, using a sling and padding for comfort. Putting it back can damage nerves and vessels and is not an EMR skill."),
    S(0, 2, "Seizure", "A man's seizure has stopped. He is breathing but drowsy. How should you position him?",
      ["On his side", "Sitting up in a chair", "Flat with legs raised", "Face down"],
      "Once the jerking stops, roll him onto his side so saliva or vomit can drain, and watch his breathing until he wakes."),
    S(0, 2, "Behavioral emergency", "Under the national model, which response to a combative patient is outside EMR scope?",
      ["Mechanical restraint", "Calling law enforcement", "Leaving to a safe distance", "Talking in a calm voice"],
      "The 2019 national model lists mechanical patient restraint at the EMT level and above. An EMR keeps a safe distance, talks calmly and calls police if the patient becomes violent."),
    S(0, 2, "Bee sting", "A bee stinger is still in a man's forearm. How should you remove it?",
      ["Scrape or pull it out quickly", "Leave it for the hospital", "Squeeze the skin around it hard", "Cut the skin to lift it out"],
      "A stinger left in place can keep delivering venom, so remove it promptly without squeezing the venom sac. Then watch for an allergic reaction."),
    M(0, 2, "Cardiac arrest", "Which actions improve survival in adult cardiac arrest? Select two.",
      ["Starting compressions quickly", "Using an AED as soon as it arrives", "Checking the pulse for a full minute", "Pausing compressions to find a vein", "Giving breaths before every compression"],
      "Early, high-quality compressions and early defibrillation are what most improve survival. Long pulse checks and other pauses take blood flow away."),
    X(0, 2, "Oxygen devices", "Choose the usual flow range for each device.",
      ["Nasal cannula", "Nonrebreather mask"],
      ["1 to 6 L/min", "10 to 15 L/min"], [0, 1],
      "A nasal cannula runs at 1 to 6 L/min. A nonrebreather needs 10 to 15 L/min to keep its reservoir bag inflated."),
    S(0, 2, "Lifting", "How should you lift a heavy patient on a device with your partner?",
      ["Bend at the knees and keep your back straight", "Bend at the waist and lift fast", "Twist to face the door as you lift, to save time", "Hold the weight far out from your body"],
      "Use your legs, keep your back straight, hold the load close and avoid twisting. Ask for more help when a patient is too heavy."),
    S(0, 2, "Evisceration", "Loops of bowel stick out of a stab wound in the belly. How should you cover them?",
      ["With a moist sterile dressing", "Push them back inside first", "With a dry, sticky bandage", "Leave them uncovered"],
      "Never push organs back in. Cover them with a sterile dressing moistened with sterile saline, then a covering to keep them warm, and treat for shock."),
    S(0, 2, "Child seat", "A toddler was in a car seat during a minor crash and is awake and calm. How might you limit movement?",
      ["Keep the child in the seat if it is intact", "Take the child out and hold him", "Have him walk to the ambulance", "Strap him upright to an adult board"],
      "If the car seat is undamaged and the child is stable, the seat itself can limit movement with padding added. Remove the child only if the seat is damaged or care requires it."),
    S(0, 3, "Bariatric patient", "A very heavy patient must be moved from an upstairs bedroom. What is the best plan?",
      ["Call for more lifting help and equipment", "Lift him alone to save time and space", "Have him slide down the stairs on a blanket", "Leave him there and treat in place until he improves"],
      "Moving a heavy patient safely takes enough people and the right equipment. Ask early for lift assistance so no one, patient or rescuer, is injured."),
]
